 toronto protest  actually has nothing to do with them  that those social media posts were spam  or misdirects so the big question here  is how is toronto police getting its  intel here and what is police expecting  to see in terms of what this anticipated  protest will actually look like who will  be there and if it will include large  vehicles like trucks or not  well thank you for the question  the first thing i will say is that our  intelligence is not based merely on  social media posts we have a very  elaborate and robust intelligence  operation that incorporates the entire  province and all the agencies within  that province  at both the municipal provincial and  federal level so our intelligence is  actually quite robust  i'm not going to go into specifically  what it is but uh  i can assure you that is very robust  thank you and chris do you have a  follow-up  yeah i do this one is for the chief and  also uh mayor tory if you could weigh in  on this one as well um the question here  is what is an acceptable protest first  when is it time to step in i mean if  protesters are trying to dig in and stay  here like we've seen in windsor and in  ottawa what's the threshold or the  timeline for when police and local  authorities will step in and stop it  well perhaps i should start with that uh  what i will say to you is that we are  here to preserve public safety and  facilitate peaceful protest and peaceful  protest does not include vehicles  jamming up the roadways so  we will facilitate any type of protest  tomorrow as we do week in and week out  throughout the year uh that one that  people want to do that protest  peacefully but again where i can tell  you from and that's why we've engaged in  the activities we've had that we are  doing and are currently doing to not  have the roadways and seats of  government jammed up and occupied so  that's what we're attempting to stop and  will not be tolerated  i'll just say that i think a lot of  people a lot of the time have a very uh  have a hard time understanding the  difficulty that's involved in the  discretion that is exercised by all  police everywhere in carrying over  duties because they precisely have to  answer a question just like that as to  when it is enough too much  but i i will say  that i think when we see instances where  and i've talked a lot this week about  the fact that there are many sets of  rights that have to be taken into  account yes there are the rights of  people to peaceful and hopefully  respectful protests and the words  peaceful and respectful are an important  part of that but those have to be you  know weighed against and it's part of  the difficulty that police have to  engage in day in and day out the rights  of other people to go about their  business to go to work to go to the  hospital without harassment to go to  work at the hospital without harassment  to just move about their city to live in  peace and quiet  and so  there there comes a time you know and  it's hard to measure whether it's  minutes hours or days there comes a time  when it's just too much  and i think as i said earlier when  premier ford declared the state of  emergency today it's time some people  would say it was time before now but  it's certainly time now and uh i think  that is part of what the police have to  decide and they're very difficult and  important responsibilities  thank you austin delaney from ctp news  do you have a question  i do it's for the chief uh chief what is  your intelligence telling you what kind  of numbers can we expect are you looking  at people number of people are you  looking at trucks number of trucks  oh i i'm not going to really discuss the  specific intelligence austin but we are  preparing for every eventuality and we  have a very robust plan  that uh is very nimble and allows us to  scale up or scale down whatever is  required  and if chief if they start to look like  they're going to park and stay for a  while um what's the plan there do you  plan on towing them to how will you move  people out we're going to help them  leave  and what does that mean  well we put we've as we have assets in  place to make sure if people are  if vehicles are engaged and they're  they're they're not moving we're going  to move them out of the way  thank you austin  next we have wendy gillis from the star  wendy go ahead with your question  thank you so much this question is for a  staff superintendent poke as well as to  framer i'm just wondering how you are  sorting through sort of the  misinformation that might be coming your  way around uh threats of arrival of  protesters how are you determining  determining what's legitimate and and  what's you know sort of a part of a game  as some of the organizers have said uh  this week  thank you wendy uh again as the chief  has says i i said i don't really want to  comment on specific intelligence but i  can tell you that we certainly uh we  look at all of the intelligence coming  in and we certainly uh try to source  that uh for credibility um as well as  working with uh all of the law  enforcement partners across the province  as well to determine uh you know what we  what to expect here and again as the  chief said we do have a plan and uh we  plan for many different uh scenarios and  we're prepared for that  actually wendy i will comment it and  it's actually a very good question there  is we know deliberately a lot of  disinformation misinformation out there  that very much complicates the province  it is an organized group  of people um that are trying to make it  difficult for police agencies across  across the province we believe some of  them are deliberately trying to tax our  resources so  uh we have to weigh all the information  the best we can with all our partners  and do the best we can and that that's  what we're trying to do but you're  absolutely right that that strategy is  out there  go ahead could you follow us one day  thank you thank you so much for that  answer and and you did mention chief  just sort of drawing on resources i am  wondering if you can give us a sense of  what impact this is having on on  deployment i'm sure officers are being  pulled off their regular assignments can  you give us any sense of what the impact  this is happening having  yes we're uh another good question wendy  we are able to maintain our business  continuity uh throughout the city  we are  redeploying a plainclothes assets that  are now into uniform we of course have  people on overtime and callbacks we have  a lot of city assets that are assisting  us so it really is an all hands on deck  at all ranks civilians as well that are  assisting us in these operations you'll  notice our  uh you know our fleet services several  of our uh city of our toronto police  service tow trucks are out there as well  so we can move very very quickly if need  be so it really is an all hands on deck  approach but uh business continuity is  being in pain is being maintained uh and  we're collaborating very much with our  again our  our partners across the province and in  particular our gta partners  thank you next up we have mark  mcallister for city news mark do you  have a question  i do thank you it's for chief ramer as  well  you had said basically  we'll simply move them if they get in  the way i'm just curious  what that involves  exactly we're also saying subject to  enforcement so  what does enforcement mean how do you  move them  we have uh extremely well trained public  order personnel and op and and  commanders on the ground that will make  those determinations as the situation  develops uh what i will say is that we  are not going to allow encampments  that's priority one  well i shouldn't say it's prior it's  it's  our priority is to maintain the safety  of all the public and our hospitals and  that's why and the seats of government  but in terms of our strategies the  priority will be to allow peaceful  protests but not allow encampments and  to occupy roadways  do you have a follow-up mom  i do thank you  aside from what has been done to close  down the streets in and around queen's  park  are you prepared for anything happening  elsewhere in the city and what exactly  are you preparing for  we do have a perimeter uh that we will  shut down if need be in the downtown  core that that is primarily looking at  the the queen's park and city hall of  course and and some adjoining areas and  that will be our focus in the downtown  core to protect that area and to make  sure it's not occupied but again  even in within that area we will  facilitate peaceful protest  thank you next up we have mark douglas  from 680 mark do you have a question  uh thank you connie question for chief  raymer  we've heard some comments with new state  of emergency in ontario do you expect  the state of emergency and the crackdown  measure that it allows will make for a  reduced protest in toronto this weekend  or could it possibly just make the  convoy demonstrators mad and enslave the  whole situation  i i think it was an important decision  i'm pleased with the decision to enact  the state of emergency i mean we're  assessing the tools um that have been uh  given to us and and you know during the  course of any demonstration or protest  we'll evaluate and determine whether any  of them need to be implemented based on  what is happening but i can tell you  that anything that we do in that regard  will only be if  if necessary  would you rather follow up  yes thank you uh question for mayor  torrey you mentioned how common how  common it is to see protests in toronto  i think anyone who lives in toronto  would agree with you but i'd like to  know  just how you're feeling about all this  did you ever imagine you would see in  toronto such an extreme reaction as what  we've been seeing with convoy  demonstrators as they respond to what  they don't like about the the copen and  the pandemic protection measures is is 