  police officers on the ground uh we did  move in and ask those uh protesters and  uh truck drivers to leave and we were we  did receive cooperation we did have  heavy toes in the area uh if we were  faced with any resistance but we were  able to clear the area and that's why  i've asked again  uh this weekend to have that cooperation  again  thank you  and you have a follow-up katie  yeah the protesters were able to learn a  lot  from police last weekend in terms of  what your mitigation efforts are  how do you plan for that for any kind of  counter planning that that protesters  might have to sort of circumvent the the  barriers and the planning measures that  you put in place  um  good question but i think i'll start  with saying that uh we have better  planners  and um  uh that's precisely what's involved we  have a very robust plan and some good  people so you asked about uh earlier  about a a learning point well one of the  things we learned about avenue road and  bloor is that we had small tourist  police vehicles there and there was a  concern at one point in time that the  large trucks were going to try to move  them out of the way slightly so they  could move further well you'll see now  that there are no small tourist vehicles  parked at avenue road in bloor and that  perimeter will expand so yeah we do  learn things when we maybe try to decide  what they are capable of or prepared to  do and so all of those things are taking  account into our planning and i'll just  emphasize again it's very adaptable it's  very nimble and i'm quite confident  we're prepared for any eventuality  thank you  our final  go to scott laurie at the toronto sun  scott do you have a question  uh yes i do thanks uh question for uh  chief ramer it's only been a few hours  since uh state of emergency was declared  uh by the premier what kind of  discussions have you had about  how that state of emergency  will  affect the response this weekend  that's a good question one of the fi